# Do NOT add a bare `build/` rule here. It looks like ordinary cleanup, and it silently
# re-ignores the carve-out made at the top of this file (`/build*` -> `!/build/` -> `/build/*`
# -> `!/build/bin/` -> `!/build/bin/*`), because the LAST matching pattern wins. That is exactly
# what had happened: the carve-out was already correct, a later `build/` overrode it, and the
# pre-commit gate (scripts/check_build_tracked.sh) then reported all 132 build/bin artifacts as
# "untracked dylib" -- a build-artifact policy that existed on paper and was defeated in fact.
# If the intent is "ignore the ~900 MB of object files + CMake state", the carve-out's
# `/build/*` line already does that; build/bin would not be tracked without its negations.
#
